# nr-relay-ws: the WebSocket server of the relay (mixdog-relay src/ws/{mod,handshake,codec,socket}.rs, tests/ws.rs).
# Nothing here could be compiled (no cargo in this task). Written against the crate's current Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock.

## 1. native/core/crates/mixdog-relay/Cargo.toml
[dev-dependencies], the tokio line: add the feature "process" (tests/ws.rs starts the Node fixture with tokio::process::Command, as
mixdog-core-review and mixdog-mcp do). The line becomes:
    tokio = { workspace = true, features = ["rt", "rt-multi-thread", "macros", "io-util", "sync", "time", "test-util", "process"] }
Nothing else: bytes, sha1, base64, httparse, serde_json, thiserror, tokio-rustls and tokio-tungstenite (its re-exported `tungstenite`
client, sync, used by one test) are already dependencies of the crate. No new package, no Cargo.lock change.

## 2. native/core/Cargo.lock and mixdog-workspace-hack
No change.

## 3. compose_flip.rs SERVICE_PENDING
No protocol method is registered by this task: nothing to remove.

## 4. What the tasks that use the ws layer must do (nr-relay-http1, nr-relay-legs, nr-relay-server)
- The hand-off from the HTTP layer is `WebSocketServer::handle_upgrade(&HandshakeRequest, io, leftover) -> Result<(WebSocket,
  WsEvents), Refused>`. `HandshakeRequest { method, headers: Vec<(name, value)> }` is built from the parsed request head (arrival
  order, any case; repeated headers are joined with ", " by the lookup, as Node's req.headers does). `io` is any `ws::Transport`
  (AsyncRead + AsyncWrite + Unpin + Send + 'static, with a default no-op `set_no_delay`): TcpStream and the tokio-rustls server
  stream are implemented here; the http1 task implements the trait for its own connection type (calling the real set_nodelay).
  `leftover` is the bytes read past the request head. A request that is refused gets ws's own answer and the connection is ended
  and dropped; `Refused::Status(n)` tells the caller which.
- Reading starts when the first event is taken from `WsEvents::next()` (or when it is dropped, or `close`/`terminate` is called), so
  the legs install their raw tap (`WebSocket::set_raw_tap`) and holds before the first `next()`: the tap then sees the leftover bytes
  first, as `prependListener('data')` does. The tap may call `gate.hold(Holder::Ingress)` from inside `chunk`: the chunk in hand is
  still parsed, the next read waits.
- legPausedUplink / legPausedIngress are `Holder::Uplink` / `Holder::Ingress` on `WebSocket::gate()` (hold, release, is_held); the
  transport is read again only when both are released and `pause()` was not called.
- send callbacks are `FlushCallback = Box<dyn FnOnce(Result<(), SendError>) + Send>`, run on the writer task (or a fresh task when
  the socket is not open): they must not block. `buffered_amount()` is the bytes (headers included) queued and not yet accepted by the
  transport; like ws, a send on a socket that is not open adds its length to it for good and fails its callback.
- A close reason is bytes (`impl AsRef<[u8]>`): a parsed (JSON) string must go through `js::node_utf8` first. `close` returns Err for
  a code ws would not send (1004-1006 and outside 1000-1014 / 3000-4999) and for a reason over 123 bytes, sending nothing.
- Events end with `WsEvent::Close { code, reason }`; code is 1006 unless the peer's close frame was read. `WebSocketServer::clients()`
  is `wss.clients`; `close().await` is `wss.close()` (new upgrades answer 503, returns when the last socket closed).
- Not ported because the relay never uses it: client mode, permessage-deflate, verifyClient, handleProtocols, `path`, wsClientError,
  autoPong off, binaryType other than Buffer, Blob data. One difference from ws 8.21.3: the receiver limits of ws 8.22 (a message of
  more than 16384 fragments, 256K unread chunks) are present, as in the installed ws the twin test runs against.

## 5. Tests
tests/ws.rs needs `node` on PATH and the `ws` package resolvable from the checkout (the root node_modules). It starts
tests/fixtures/node-ws-echo.mjs (a Node process on 127.0.0.1) and nothing else.
